From c3490c91769e76cdc88dd45763f420fab8301c93 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 20:42:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] kanban/state.db: kept read connections for
 background readers (card I)

Change-proportional design t_01a1d563324d7e8e, card I. In the hub gateway profile
(prof10-gw-434368) connection opens were 11.4% of samples: connect/_open_configured,
kanban_generation.prepare_connection, the lockguard _apply fd scan, and the writable
SessionDB opens of the remote-notice sweep.

- hermes_cli/kanban_kept_reader.py: `reading()` is a drop-in for
  `connect_closing` in read-only callers. It keeps one query_only connection per
  (thread, board file), so prepare_connection and the OFD lock guard run once per
  kept connection, not once per read. The connection is reopened through
  `connect()` when the board's (dev, ino) changes, its -shm identity changes (a
  new WAL generation), the path drops out of _INITIALIZED_PATHS
  (init/repair/lost schema), the generation is poisoned, or recovery closed it.
  It is registered with kanban_generation.register_reader_cache, so recovery can
  retire an idle kept connection that sits on another thread. A borrowed
  connection is never closed under its borrower; recovery is told to retry.
  Nested borrows, fenced boards and read-only-schema boards get a plain
  per-call connection.
- First caller: kanban_decompose.list_triage_ids, which runs on every dispatcher
  pass for every board.
- hermes_state_kept_reader.py: one kept SessionDB(read_only=True) per state.db
  path per process. It runs no schema reconcile and is reopened on a change of
  file or sidecar identity, a close, a quarantine, or a DatabaseError in the
  body. hermes_state_registry.close_all/close_all_under retire these handles too.
- tui_gateway/kanban_notice_session_store.py + kanban_remote_notices._session_db:
  the remote-notice pass reads on the kept read-only handle. Only the notice write
  borrows the shared registry writer (notice_writer).
- kanban_db_connect.connect gains check_same_thread (default True). Kept readers
  are the only callers that pass False.

The lockguard fd scan is unchanged (C14): no verdict cache. It runs less often
only because there are fewer opens.

Tests (RED on main call sites, GREEN here):
- tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_kept_reader.py: list_triage_ids prepares one
  connection across 5 passes (main: 5); a file replaced under a kept connection
  reopens and closes the stale handle; dropped init state reopens; cross-thread
  recovery eviction; a borrowed connection is not evicted; nested borrow;
  sqlite error drops the connection; an exited thread's connections are closed.
- tests/hermes_state/test_state_kept_reader.py: the kept handle is reused and
  runs no _init_schema (main _session_db: once per pass); a replaced file reopens;
  close, DatabaseError and registry close_all_under retire the handle; a notice
  write goes through the shared writer.
